## The idea

Make **every ticket one entry** in a list: a uniform pick over tickets is then automatically weighted by member. Removing from the middle would be O(n), so **move the last ticket into the hole** and drop the last slot. To find a member's tickets in O(1), keep member → set of positions.

## Step by step

1. AddTicket: record the new position in the member's set; append the member id.
2. RemoveTicket: take any position p of this member; move the last ticket to p and update its owner's set; remove the last slot and p from this member's set.
3. GetRandom: tickets[random index]; -1 if empty.
4. Follow-up: every operation touches two structures, so one lock around each.

## Worked example

**Input:** add 7 ×2, add 9, remove one of 7's

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| after adds | tickets [7, 7, 9]; 7 → {0, 1}, 9 → {2} |
| remove 7 (position 0) | move 9 to slot 0 → [9, 7]; 9 → {0}; 7 → {1} |
| the trap | if the last ticket is ALSO 7's, remove position 0 from 7's set before adding the moved ticket's new position — the other order loses a ticket |
| GetRandom | 7 or 9, each 1 in 2 |

**Result:** tickets [9, 7] — no gaps, still weighted correctly

## Cost

O(1) average for all three; O(total tickets) memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LinkedIn (reported Oct 2026) — a fair: AddTicket / RemoveTicket / GetRandom weighted by tickets, all O(1) average.
// Close to LeetCode 381 (Insert Delete GetRandom O(1) — duplicates allowed): each ticket is one entry.
public sealed class TicketRaffle {
    private readonly List<int> _tickets = new List<int>();                      // one entry per ticket: the member id
    private readonly Dictionary<int, HashSet<int>> _where = new Dictionary<int, HashSet<int>>();   // member -> positions
    private readonly Random _rnd;
    private readonly object _gate = new object();                               // follow-up: many threads

    public TicketRaffle() : this(new Random()) { }
    public TicketRaffle(Random rnd) { _rnd = rnd; }

    public void AddTicket(int memberId) {
        lock (_gate) {
            if (!_where.TryGetValue(memberId, out var set)) _where[memberId] = set = new HashSet<int>();
            set.Add(_tickets.Count);
            _tickets.Add(memberId);
        }
    }

    // Takes one ticket away. False if the member has none.
    public bool RemoveTicket(int memberId) {
        lock (_gate) {
            if (!_where.TryGetValue(memberId, out var set) || set.Count == 0) return false;
            int pos = 0;
            foreach (int p in set) { pos = p; break; }          // any one of this member's tickets
            int lastPos = _tickets.Count - 1, lastMember = _tickets[lastPos];
            set.Remove(pos);                                    // first: this ticket is gone
            if (pos != lastPos) {
                // Move the last ticket into the hole: O(1). Its owner may be this same member, which is why
                // `pos` had to leave the set BEFORE the moved ticket's new position is added.
                _tickets[pos] = lastMember;
                _where[lastMember].Remove(lastPos);
                _where[lastMember].Add(pos);
            }
            _tickets.RemoveAt(lastPos);
            if (set.Count == 0) _where.Remove(memberId);
            return true;
        }
    }

    // A member chosen with probability (their tickets / all tickets). -1 when nobody has a ticket.
    public int GetRandom() {
        lock (_gate) {
            if (_tickets.Count == 0) return -1;
            return _tickets[_rnd.Next(_tickets.Count)];      // every ticket equally likely → members by weight
        }
    }

    public int TicketsOf(int memberId) {
        lock (_gate) return _where.TryGetValue(memberId, out var set) ? set.Count : 0;
    }

    public int Total { get { lock (_gate) return _tickets.Count; } }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Why a ticket per entry?**

A uniform pick over tickets is a weighted pick over members — no prefix sums needed.

**Q: RemoveTicket the last ticket in the list?**

The "moved" ticket is the removed one: update the set before deleting, or handle pos == last.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(1) average for all three; O(total tickets) memory.

**Q: Memory per member instead of per ticket?**

Then picks need prefix sums: a Fenwick tree gives O(log n) for everything.

**Q: Many threads.**

Each operation touches two structures, so one lock around each; Random is not thread-safe either.

**Q: A billion tickets across services.**

Shard by member; pick a shard by its total tickets, then pick inside it — two-level weighting.

